[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
Below is a **step‑by‑step checklist** that will turn your “camouflaged” green‑/brown‑filled point symbols into icons that stay readable on any satellite or aerial background.  
Everything is drawn from the *MapLibre‑Cartography* skill, so you can copy‑paste the snippets straight into your style or JavaScript code.

---

## 1. The core idea – give every icon a **contrast halo** (or outline)

* On imagery there is no single background colour, so the only reliable way to separate a symbol from the photo is to **add a thin, semi‑transparent ring that is the opposite of the background**.  
* The halo works exactly like a text halo: it is drawn *outside* the icon shape, so the icon’s colour can stay saturated (amber, terracotta, teal…) while the halo supplies the figure‑ground separation.

### 1.1. Use an **SDF sprite** (Signed‑Distance‑Field)

Only SDF images can be given a halo via the style properties `icon-halo-color` and `icon-halo-width`. If you are currently using raster PNG icons, you have two options:

| Option | When to use | How |
|--------|-------------|-----|
| **Convert your SVGs to SDF** (recommended) | You control the source SVGs, you want a single colour that you can recolour at runtime. | `map.addImage('my‑icon', svgImage, { sdf: true })`  (see code below). |
| **Bake the halo into the PNG** | You have a handful of static icons and don’t want a build step. | Add a 1‑2 px outline in the image editor, make it a contrasting colour (usually dark for bright icons, light for dark icons). |

> **Why SDF?**  
> * The halo colour/width can be changed in the style without re‑creating the sprite.  
> * You can recolour the whole icon with `icon-color` (e.g. keep the same base shape for “park”, “school”, “hospital” and just switch the fill colour).  

---

## 2. Build a minimal SDF sprite set

### 2.1. Create the SVG (example)

```svg
<!-- my‑icon.svg – keep the viewBox square and add a 2‑pixel padding -->
<svg viewBox="-2 -2 20 20" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
  <!-- the shape – use a single solid colour (black) that will be recoloured later -->
  <path d="M10 0 L12 6 L18 6 L13 10 L15 16 L10 12 L5 16 L7 10 L2 6 L8 6 Z"
        fill="black"/>
</svg>
```

*The black fill is the “source colour”.* When you add it as an SDF image you can later set `icon-color` to any hex you like.

### 2.2. Load it at runtime (no sprite‑sheet needed for a few icons)

```js
// Helper – fetch an SVG, rasterise it, then add as an SDF image
async function addSdfIcon(map, id, url) {
  const resp = await fetch(url);
  const svgText = await resp.text();

  // Create an Image element from the SVG string
  const img = new Image();
  const svgBlob = new Blob([svgText], {type: 'image/svg+xml'});
  img.src = URL.createObjectURL(svgBlob);

  await new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    img.onload = () => resolve();
    img.onerror = (e) => reject(e);
  });

  // Add to the map as an SDF image
  map.addImage(id, img, { sdf: true, pixelRatio: 2 }); // pixelRatio optional
}

// Usage
map.on('load', async () => {
  await addSdfIcon(map, 'marker-geo', '/icons/marker.svg');

  // Now you can reference "marker-geo" in any symbol layer
});
```

> **Tip:** If you have dozens of icons, generate a sprite sheet with `spritezero` or `spreet`. The JSON index must contain the same IDs you use in `icon-image`.

---

## 3. Style the icon layer – halo, colour, shadow, and ordering

```json
{
  "id": "my-points",
  "type": "symbol",
  "source": "my-points-source",
  "layout": {
    "icon-image": "marker-geo",          // the SDF id you added
    "icon-size": ["interpolate", ["linear"], ["zoom"],
                  10, 0.6,
                  14, 1.2],
    "icon-allow-overlap": true,
    "icon-ignore-placement": false
  },
  "paint": {
    // 1️⃣ Choose a saturated “earth‑tone” that is NOT common in the imagery.
    //    Amber, terracotta, teal, or a vivid magenta work well.
    "icon-color": "#ff7e00",               // example: bright amber

    // 2️⃣ Add a dark semi‑transparent halo – this is the figure‑ground cue.
    "icon-halo-color": "rgba(0,0,0,0.75)",
    "icon-halo-width": 1.2,                // 1‑2 px works for most zooms

    // 3️⃣ Optional: a thin inner outline that matches the halo colour.
    //    This helps when the icon is very small.
    "icon-outline-color": "rgba(0,0,0,0.5)",
    "icon-outline-width": 0.5,

    // 4️⃣ Optional: a subtle “contact shadow” that anchors the icon to the ground.
    //    This is a drop‑shadow that is offset downwards and blurred.
    "icon-translate": [0, 2],              // shift down 2 px (pixel units)
    "icon-translate-anchor": "map",
    "icon-opacity": 1.0
  }
}
```

### Why each property matters

| Property | What it does | Recommended value for imagery |
|----------|--------------|------------------------------|
| **`icon-color`** | Base fill of the SDF shape. Use a **high‑chroma, earth‑tone** (amber, terracotta, teal, magenta). Avoid greens/browns that appear in the photo. | `#ff7e00` (amber) or `#d84315` (terracotta) |
| **`icon-halo-color`** | The ring that separates the icon from the background. Must be **dark** on imagery. | `rgba(0,0,0,0.75)` |
| **`icon-halo-width`** | Thickness of the ring. 1‑2 px is enough; increase only for very large icons. | `1.2` |
| **`icon-outline-color/width`** | A second, thinner outline that stays visible when the halo is thin. | `rgba(0,0,0,0.5)` / `0.5` |
| **`icon-translate`** | Gives a tiny “contact shadow” effect (the icon looks grounded). | `[0,2]` (down 2 px) |
| **`icon-allow-overlap`** | Prevents the engine from hiding icons that would otherwise collide. Use only if you really need dense clusters. | `true` (most POI layers) |
| **`icon-size`** | Scale with zoom so the halo stays proportional. | Interpolated as shown above |

> **Result:** The amber icon will pop on dark forest canopies, bright fields, water, or rooftops because the dark halo creates a constant contrast edge. The halo is semi‑transparent, so the icon still feels “attached” to the photo rather than looking like a sticker.

---

## 4. If you must keep the original green / brown fill

Sometimes the colour carries semantic meaning (e.g., green = park, brown = agriculture). You can keep the hue **and** make it legible by:

1. **Adding a *dark* halo** (as above). The halo will dominate the contrast, so the underlying hue can stay.
2. **Adding a *light* inner halo** (two‑halo technique) – first a thin dark outer halo, then a thin light inner halo that matches the icon colour. This creates a “double‑stroke” effect that works on both bright and dark backgrounds.

```json
{
  "icon-color": "#6abf40",                     // green park
  "icon-halo-color": "rgba(0,0,0,0.75)",
  "icon-halo-width": 1.5,
  "icon-outline-color": "rgba(255,255,255,0.6)",
  "icon-outline-width": 0.6
}
```

The outer dark halo guarantees separation; the inner light outline preserves the visual identity of the original colour.

---

## 5. Layer ordering – keep your icons **under** basemap labels but **above** the raster imagery

```js
// Find the first symbol layer in the base style (usually the label layer)
const firstSymbolId = map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type === 'symbol').id;

// Insert your point layer just before that layer
map.addLayer(myPointLayer, firstSymbolId);
```

If you add the layer *after* the label layer, your icons will cover the basemap text, which is the most common source of complaints.

---

## 6. Debugging checklist (quick “did‑I‑forget‑something?”)

| ✅ Check | How to verify |
|--------|----------------|
| **SDF sprite** | `map.hasImage('marker-geo')` returns `true`. |
| **Halo appears** | Zoom in/out; you should see a thin dark ring around the icon even on bright fields. |
| **Icon colour is saturated** | Use a colour picker on the rendered icon; it should be the hex you set (`icon-color`). |
| **Layer order** | In the style JSON, your point layer appears **before** the first `symbol` layer. |
| **Sprite URL** | If you use a sprite sheet, open `<sprite‑url>.json` in the browser and confirm the icon ID exists. |
| **Accessibility contrast** | Use the WebAIM Contrast Checker on the *combined* colour (icon + halo). Aim for ≥ 4.5:1 for normal text‑size icons (most icons are ≥ 12 px). |
| **Performance** | Keep `icon-size` ≤ 2 at the highest zoom; larger sizes cause heavy over‑draw on dense imagery. |

---

## 7. Full example – putting it all together

```js
map.on('load', async () => {
  // 1️⃣ Load the SDF icon
  await addSdfIcon(map, 'park-marker', '/icons/park.svg');

  // 2️⃣ Add a source (GeoJSON for demo)
  map.addSource('parks', {
    type: 'geojson',
    data: '/data/parks.geojson'
  });

  // 3️⃣ Define the symbol layer
  const parkLayer = {
    id: 'park-points',
    type: 'symbol',
    source: 'parks',
    layout: {
      'icon-image': 'park-marker',
      'icon-size': ['interpolate', ['linear'], ['zoom'], 10, 0.6, 14, 1.2],
      'icon-allow-overlap': true
    },
    paint: {
      'icon-color': '#6abf40',                     // green (semantic)
      'icon-halo-color': 'rgba(0,0,0,0.75)',       // dark halo
      'icon-halo-width': 1.2,
      'icon-outline-color': 'rgba(255,255,255,0.6)', // optional inner halo
      'icon-outline-width': 0.5,
      'icon-translate': [0, 2],
      'icon-translate-anchor': 'map'
    }
  };

  // 4️⃣ Insert before the first label layer
  const firstSymbol = map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type === 'symbol').id;
  map.addLayer(parkLayer, firstSymbol);
});
```

Result: **green park icons stay visible** on any forest, field, or rooftop background, thanks to the dark halo and optional inner light outline.

---

## 8. Quick “cheat‑sheet” for any new icon

| Step | Action |
|------|--------|
| 1️⃣ | Convert the SVG to an **SDF** image (`sdf: true`). |
| 2️⃣ | Choose a **high‑chroma base colour** that is *not* dominant in the imagery (amber, terracotta, teal, magenta). |
| 3️⃣ | Add a **dark semi‑transparent halo** (`rgba(0,0,0,0.75)`, width ≈ 1 px). |
| 4️⃣ | (Optional) Add a **light inner outline** if you need to keep the original hue. |
| 5️⃣ | Add a **tiny contact shadow** (`icon-translate`) to ground the

Below is a **complete, copy‑and‑paste ready** example that shows how to:

1. **Add a button** to the page.  
2. **Fly the camera** to a new latitude/longitude (or any other view parameters) when the button is clicked.  
3. Keep the animation **smooth and interrupt‑friendly** (a second click cancels the previous flight).  
4. Respect **accessibility** (reduced‑motion users, focus handling, keyboard‑friendly button).

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>MapLibre – smooth flyTo on button click</title>
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; height:100%; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
    /* Simple button styling – keep it reachable by keyboard */
    #flyBtn {
      position: absolute;
      top: 10px; left: 10px;
      padding: 0.5rem 1rem;
      background: #fff;
      border: 1px solid #999;
      border-radius: 4px;
      cursor: pointer;
      font-family: sans-serif;
    }
    #flyBtn:focus { outline: 2px solid #0066ff; }
  </style>
  <!-- MapLibre GL JS (use the latest version you have) -->
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet"/>
</head>
<body>

<div id="map"></div>
<button id="flyBtn">Fly to San Francisco</button>

<script>
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣ Initialise the map
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any style you like
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574], // start over San Francisco (lon, lat)
    zoom: 12,
    pitch: 0,
    bearing: 0,
    // optional: improve performance on low‑end devices
    // preserveDrawingBuffer: false,
  });

  // Wait until the style and sources are ready before we allow a fly.
  map.once('load', () => {
    // Give the button keyboard focus after the map loads (helps screen‑reader users)
    document.getElementById('flyBtn').focus();
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣ Helper: smoothFly(targetView)
  // -------------------------------------------------
  /**
   * Fly the map to a new view.
   *
   * @param {Object} view – MapLibre camera options.
   *   {Array<number>} view.center   – [lon, lat]
   *   {number}        view.zoom     – zoom level
   *   {number}        view.bearing  – rotation in degrees (optional)
   *   {number}        view.pitch    – tilt in degrees (optional)
   *   {number}        view.duration – ms (optional, default 1000)
   *   {number}        view.curve    – flight curve (default 1.42)
   *   {boolean}       view.essential – true → respects reduced‑motion
   */
  function smoothFly(view) {
    // If the user has “prefers‑reduced‑motion”, we skip the animation.
    const prefersReduced = window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches;

    // Cancel any ongoing animation – map.flyTo does this internally,
    // but calling it again while a previous flight is still in progress
    // guarantees the new target wins.
    map.stop();

    map.flyTo({
      center: view.center,
      zoom: view.zoom,
      bearing: view.bearing ?? map.getBearing(),
      pitch: view.pitch ?? map.getPitch(),
      duration: prefersReduced ? 0 : (view.duration ?? 1200),
      curve: view.curve ?? 1.42,
      // essential:true tells MapLibre to ignore the animation if the user
      // has reduced‑motion enabled. We already handled that above, but
      // keeping the flag is a good habit.
      essential: true,
      // Optional callbacks – useful for UI state (e.g., disabling the button)
      //   onStart: () => console.log('flight started'),
      //   onComplete: () => console.log('flight finished')
    });
  }

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 3️⃣ Wire the button click (or keyboard “Enter/Space”)
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const btn = document.getElementById('flyBtn');

  btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
    // Example target – Golden Gate Bridge
    smoothFly({
      center: [-122.478255, 37.819932],
      zoom: 15,
      bearing: 30,   // give a slight rotation for visual interest
      pitch: 45,     // tilt the view so the 3‑D terrain (if any) shows
      duration: 1500 // ms – longer = smoother
    });
  });

  // Make the button work with keyboard “Enter” and “Space”
  btn.addEventListener('keydown', (e) => {
    if (e.key === 'Enter' || e.key === ' ') {
      e.preventDefault(); // prevent scrolling on Space
      btn.click();
    }
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

---

## Why This Works – the cartographic & technical rationale

| Step | What it does | Cartographic / UX reasoning |
|------|--------------|-----------------------------|
| **`map.flyTo`** | Animates the camera along a smooth “great‑circle” path. | Gives the user a clear visual cue that the map is moving to a *different* area, preserving mental map continuity. |
| **`duration` / `curve`** | Controls speed and the “easing” of the flight. | A longer duration (≈ 1 – 2 s) feels natural; the default curve = 1.42 is a good compromise between a tight zoom‑in and a slow pan. |
| **`essential: true`** | Respects the user’s “prefers‑reduced‑motion” setting. | Meets WCAG 2.1 AA accessibility requirements. |
| **`map.stop()` before `flyTo`** | Cancels any in‑progress animation. | Prevents “jitter” when the user clicks the button repeatedly. |
| **Keyboard‑friendly button** | `focus`, `keydown` handling, visible focus ring. | Makes the control reachable for screen‑reader and keyboard‑only users. |
| **`bearing` & `pitch`** | Optional rotation/tilt. | Adds a sense of depth on imagery or hill‑shade basemaps, but keep them modest (≤ 45°) so labels stay readable. |
| **`map.once('load')`** | Guarantees the style is ready before we call `flyTo`. | Prevents “undefined style” errors that would otherwise abort the animation. |

---

## Common Pitfalls & How to Avoid Them

| Problem | Why it happens | Fix |
|---------|----------------|-----|
| **Labels disappear during the flight** | The target zoom is so high that label layers are filtered out (`text-max-size` or `minzoom`/`maxzoom`). | Verify the label layers have a `minzoom` ≤ your target zoom, or add a temporary `text-size` ramp that stays visible. |
| **Icon halos look wrong on imagery** | You used a light halo (`rgba(255,255,255,0.8)`) which blends into bright ground. | For imagery, always use a **dark semi‑transparent halo** (`rgba(0,0,0,0.75)`) on both labels and SDF icons. |
| **Flight feels “jumpy”** | `duration` is too short or `curve` is left at the default for a very large distance. | Increase `duration` (e.g., 1500‑2000 ms) or set `curve: 1.8` for a slower, smoother arc. |
| **Screen‑reader users miss the change** | No announcement is made after the map moves. | After `flyTo` completes (`map.on('moveend', …)`), fire a custom ARIA live region update: `document.getElementById('status').textContent = 'Map moved to San Francisco.'`. |
| **Multiple clicks queue up many flights** | `flyTo` queues animations internally. | Call `map.stop()` before each new `flyTo` (as shown) or disable the button while the flight is in progress. |

---

## Quick‑Reference API Summary

```js
map.flyTo({
  center: [lon, lat],      // required
  zoom: number,            // optional – keep within style's zoom range
  bearing: number,         // optional – 0‑360°
  pitch: number,           // optional – 0‑85°
  duration: ms,            // optional – default 1000
  curve: number,           // optional – default 1.42 (higher = slower start)
  easing: fn,              // optional – custom easing function
  essential: true,         // respects reduced‑motion
  // callbacks (optional)
  // onStart: () => {},
  // onComplete: () => {}
});
```

---

### TL;DR

*Add a button → listen for `click` → call `map.flyTo` with the desired `center`, `zoom`, `bearing`, `pitch`, and a sensible `duration/curve`.  
Make the button keyboard‑accessible, respect `prefers-reduced-motion`, and stop any ongoing animation before starting a new one.*  

That’s all you need for a smooth, cartographically‑sound camera transition in MapLibre GL JS. Happy mapping!
